---

### ✅ Step 1: Install OmniEngineer in the Jupyter server image

If you're baking this into a Docker image:

```dockerfile
# Dockerfile
RUN pip install omni-engineer  # or however OmniEngineer installs
```

Make sure it’s in the default `PATH` so a terminal can find it.

---

### ✅ Step 2: Create a small JupyterLab extension

Since you just want an **icon on the Launcher or Desktop**, and for it to **open a terminal** with OmniEngineer running, you can build a simple [prebuilt extension](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/stable/extension/extension_dev.html#prebuilt-extension).

#### 🔧 Core logic (TypeScript)

```ts
import {
  JupyterFrontEnd, JupyterFrontEndPlugin
} from '@jupyterlab/application';

import { ILauncher } from '@jupyterlab/launcher';

const plugin: JupyterFrontEndPlugin<void> = {
  id: 'omni-launcher',
  autoStart: true,
  requires: [ILauncher],
  activate: (app, launcher) => {
    launcher.add({
      category: 'Other',
      name: 'OmniEngineer',
      command: 'omni:open-terminal',
    });

    app.commands.addCommand('omni:open-terminal', {
      label: 'Launch OmniEngineer',
      execute: async () => {
        const terminal = await app.serviceManager.terminals.startNew();
        app.shell.add(app.docRegistry.getWidgetFactory('Terminal').createNew(terminal), 'main');
        app.commands.execute('terminal:send', {
          name: terminal.name,
          code: 'omni\n'
        });
      }
    });
  }
};

export default plugin;
```

This:
- Adds a button to the Launcher (and optionally desktop if you're using jupyterlab-desktop).
- Starts a new terminal.
- Automatically runs the `omni` command in it.

---

### ✅ Step 3: Bundle and install the extension

Use `jupyter labextension build` or `jupyter labextension install .` depending on your packaging style.

And in your Dockerfile or server image build:

```bash
jupyter labextension install path/to/your/omni-extension
```

