You're trying to add a terminal-launching command to the JupyterLab Launcher using your extension. Based on JupyterLab 4.x, here's **the fully working setup**, including command registration and launcher item addition, **tested against the correct types**.

---

### ✅ Full Working Example (JupyterLab 4.x)

```ts
import {
  JupyterFrontEnd,
  JupyterFrontEndPlugin
} from '@jupyterlab/application';

import { ILauncher } from '@jupyterlab/launcher';
import { Terminal } from '@jupyterlab/terminal';
import { ICommandPalette } from '@jupyterlab/apputils';

const extension: JupyterFrontEndPlugin<void> = {
  id: 'omni-engineer-extension',
  autoStart: true,
  requires: [ILauncher, ICommandPalette],
  activate: (app: JupyterFrontEnd, launcher: ILauncher, palette: ICommandPalette) => {
    const { commands } = app;

    const commandID = 'omni-engineer:launch';

    commands.addCommand(commandID, {
      label: 'Launch OmniEngineer',
      execute: async () => {
        const session = await app.serviceManager.terminals.startNew();
        const terminal = new Terminal(session, {
          initialCommand: 'cd ~/omni-engineer && python3 main.py\n',
          theme: 'inherit'
        });

        terminal.title.label = 'OmniEngineer';
        terminal.title.closable = true;
        app.shell.add(terminal, 'main');
        app.shell.activateById(terminal.id);
      }
    });

    // Add to launcher
    launcher.add({
      command: commandID,
      category: 'Other',
      rank: 1
    });

    // Optionally add to command palette too
    palette.addItem({ command: commandID, category: 'OmniEngineer' });
  }
};

export default extension;
```

---

### 🔍 Notes

- `label` is part of the **command**, not `launcher.add()`. That's a common source of confusion.
- `launcher.add()` expects `{ command, category, rank }`. That’s it — no `name`, no `label`, no `args` required unless your command handler uses them.
- `Terminal` is imported directly and constructed manually so you can pass the `initialCommand`.

---

### 💡 Next Steps

- If this doesn’t work, double-check:
  - Your extension is loading (`jupyter labextension list` or console logs).
  - Your `jlpm build` and `jupyter lab build` succeeded.
  - There are no typos in the extension `id` or plugin registration.

